{"park":"denali","fetched":"2026-09-07T00:54:35+00:00","attribution":{"landmarks":"© OpenStreetMap contributors, ODbL","summaries":"Wikipedia, CC BY-SA 4.0","boundary":"iNaturalist place 71077"},"landmarks":[{"id":"node/2748929230","name":"Denali","kind":"peak","lon":-151.00624,"lat":63.06911,"ele_m":6190.0,"wikidata":"Q130018","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Denali","tour":3,"summary":{"extract":"Denali, federally designated as Mount McKinley, is the highest mountain peak in North America, with a summit elevation of 20,310 feet (6,190 m) above sea level. Its vertical relief above the surrounding plateaus is one of the greatest on Earth, measuring about 18,000 feet. With a topographic prominence of 20,156 feet (6,144 m) and a topographic isolation of 4,621.1 miles (7,436.9 km), Denali is the third most prominent and third-most isolated peak on Earth, after Mount Everest and Aconcagua. Located in the Alaska Range in the interior of the U.S. state of Alaska, Denali is the centerpiece of Denali National Park and Preserve.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Denali","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Arctic_warriors_summit_North_America%27s_highest_point%2C_put_Army_gear_to_the_test_140615-A-ZZ999-328.jpg/960px-Arctic_warriors_summit_North_America%27s_highest_point%2C_put_Army_gear_to_the_test_140615-A-ZZ999-328.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arctic_warriors_summit_North_America%27s_highest_point,_put_Army_gear_to_the_test_140615-A-ZZ999-328.jpg","dist_m":15,"width":640,"height":496,"license":"Public domain","license_url":"","artist":"Airborne"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Denali%2C_AK%2C_USA_-_panoramio.jpg/960px-Denali%2C_AK%2C_USA_-_panoramio.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Denali,_AK,_USA_-_panoramio.jpg","dist_m":38,"width":640,"height":480,"license":"CC BY 3.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0","artist":"Michael Boyd"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Alaska_Monument_of_Soldiers.jpg/960px-Alaska_Monument_of_Soldiers.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alaska_Monument_of_Soldiers.jpg","dist_m":40,"width":640,"height":640,"license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","artist":"TheCoolPinata22"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Herbert_Hellmuth_Summit_Denali.jpg/960px-Herbert_Hellmuth_Summit_Denali.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herbert_Hellmuth_Summit_Denali.jpg","dist_m":54,"width":640,"height":480,"license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","artist":"8848hehe"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/At_Mt_McKinley_Summit.JPG/960px-At_Mt_McKinley_Summit.JPG","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:At_Mt_McKinley_Summit.JPG","dist_m":75,"width":640,"height":424,"license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","artist":"Virender Singh Malik"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Mount_McKinley_or_Denali_Marna_%2838862750025%29.jpg/960px-Mount_McKinley_or_Denali_Marna_%2838862750025%29.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_McKinley_or_Denali_Marna_(38862750025).jpg","dist_m":362,"width":640,"height":480,"license":"CC BY 2.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","artist":"Iain Cameron"}],"street":null},{"id":"node/4128400291","name":"Kahiltna Dome","kind":"peak","lon":-151.23935,"lat":63.05438,"ele_m":3783.0,"wikidata":"Q14680087","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kahiltna_Dome","tour":2,"summary":{"extract":"Kahiltna Dome is a 12,411-foot (3,783 m) mountain in the central Alaska Range, in Denali National Park, 7.3 mi (12 km) west of Denali. It is separated from Denali by a deep glacial valley occupied by Kahiltna Glacier, with Kahiltna Pass at its head. It is described as an ice-covered dome, the 56th-highest peak in Alaska.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kahiltna_Dome","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[],"street":null},{"id":"node/4557218985","name":"Mount Mather","kind":"peak","lon":-150.43639,"lat":63.19472,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q14680156","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Mather_(Alaska)","tour":4,"summary":{"extract":"Mount Mather is a 12,096-foot (3,687 m) mountain in the Alaska Range, in Denali National Park and Preserve. Mount Mather lies to the northeast of Denali, overlooking Brooks Glacier. The mountain itself is covered by glaciers. Mount Mather was named in 1947 in honor of National Park Service director Stephen Mather.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Mather_(Alaska)","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[],"street":null},{"id":"node/2039527864","name":"Mount Russell","kind":"peak","lon":-151.88448,"lat":62.79825,"ele_m":3560.0,"wikidata":"Q6923367","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Russell_(Alaska)","tour":0,"summary":{"extract":"Mount Russell is one of the major peaks of the central Alaska Range, approximately 35 mi (56 km) southwest of Denali. Though much lower than Denali or its neighbor Mount Foraker, Russell is a steep, dramatic peak and a significant mountaineering challenge in its own right. To give a sense of its size and steepness, note that its summit rises 6,560 ft (2,000 m) over the Chedotlothna Glacier to the northwest in only 1.8 mi (2.9 km), and almost 10,000 ft (3,000 m) above the lower Yentna Glacier to the south in only 8 mi (13 km).","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Russell_(Alaska)","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[],"street":null},{"id":"node/4512262358","name":"Avalanche Spire","kind":"peak","lon":-151.15362,"lat":62.80636,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q8536879","url":null,"tour":1,"photos":[]